#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125726 :tutorial/content "The **gradient** of a multivariable function is analogous to the derivative of a single-variable function.\n\nFor a function $f(x,y)$ whose partial derivatives exist at a point $(x,y),$ the **gradient vector** is denoted $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and can be found by computing the vector of partial derivatives:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f (x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\mathbf i + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\mathbf j\n                              \n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b8c5c84-83d9-55aa-bb25-87cf1904ed2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2399 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125727 :tutorial/content "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector  \n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k.\n                             \n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n![](images/21/21e05d85506da576e293bce86b1ca98f4f5b101c49b0591a49d80d2a4b8bfcc1.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "be2e3466-803d-5c6c-aeb9-f6cc57b000f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2400 :tutorial/title "The Gradient of a Function of More Than Two Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125728 :tutorial/content "Since the gradient of a function of several variables is made up of partial derivatives, we can expect it to have properties that are similar to partial derivatives.\n\nAssume that $f$ and $g$ are functions whose first-order partial derivatives exist and are continuous on some open domain, and that $a,b,$ and $c$ are real constants. Then, the gradient has the following properties:\n\n- Constant rule:  $\\nabla c = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- Linearity: $\\nabla (af + bg) = a \\nabla\\! f + b\\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Product Rule: $\\nabla(fg) = g \\nabla\\! f + f \\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Quotient Rule: $\\nabla \\left( \\dfrac{f}{g}\\right) = \\dfrac{ g \\nabla\\! f - f \\nabla\\! g}{g^2}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "52036b8f-4d6f-5609-bbcf-fc34a51d9b98" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2401 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125806 :tutorial/content "For a multivariable function, the existence of the gradient implies continuity.\n\nA sufficient condition for the gradient ![](images/3a/3a172b4fd0180b172579ebac50f73d8dfb75a219a02b610f666d1a4e446592a2.svg) to exist is that the partial derivatives ![](images/f2/f20807159d7e3d172a1ed9d92b8001a4c186e78ef3b8423592c75e44b633ec88.svg) and ![](images/f4/f423cce200533b35d0f6df2d4a6f5e917529276e749a7ab4995930d64559c98d.svg) both exist in a neighborhood of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and are continuous at ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg).\n\nTherefore, if ![](images/f2/f20807159d7e3d172a1ed9d92b8001a4c186e78ef3b8423592c75e44b633ec88.svg) and ![](images/f4/f423cce200533b35d0f6df2d4a6f5e917529276e749a7ab4995930d64559c98d.svg) both exist in a neighborhood of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and are continuous at ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg), then ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) is continuous at ![](images/85/853e57b0bf9a8f7c16e59d37575ae23ac4028ed66b30720af9d7d49ab595c91b.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The continuity of the partial derivatives is crucial. The existence of the partial derivatives alone does not imply that a function is continuous at a point." :tutorial/id #uuid "652f49b9-1a78-58d1-9531-1e115634f86a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3864 :tutorial/title "Continuous Partial Derivatives Imply Continuity"}]}
